// `(T-Absent)`: `t == null` and `t ?? d` are ONE question answered from one home, so a tuple
// that answers the first by its members must DISCHARGE by them too — in whichever home it
// lives.  The boxed spelling did not: the coalesce typed its result from the boxed subject, so
// a stack default had nowhere to go — *"`??` default of type `(integer, text)` is not
// assignable to `__tuple<integer?,text?>`"* — for a pair of types that are the same tuple, one
// `?` per member apart (D-tup-10).
//
// The arm that answers the pair already existed: when the subject is boxed and the default is a
// stack literal, take the stack spelling, because the value unboxes.  What declined was its
// GATE — `unboxes_stored_tuple` demands the record's members be `is_equal` to the destination's
// elements.  The result now takes the record's OWN members in stack spelling,
// `(integer?, text?)`, which is what the stack home already produces for the same notion, so
// the two homes agree rather than one inventing an answer.  Typing it as the NON-null stack
// tuple would have been unsound in the other direction: the subject would then unbox nullable
// members into non-null elements, which `(N-Store)` refuses.
//
// ⚠ This lives in its own file rather than beside the `== null` cells ON PURPOSE.  Its cells do
// not COMPILE on the build they were written to catch, and a compile refusal takes the whole
// file with it — so holding them next to the assertion cells of the earlier close erased that
// close's assertion channel and left it with an exit-only receipt.  A cell that can refuse the
// file silences every cell behind it.
//
// b1/b2 are the fix; c1 is the STACK home, which always worked and must keep working; c2 is the
// generic `-> T?` route, which always worked because it boxes `__tuple<integer,text>` with
// NON-null members, so the gate's equality held there and never declined.
//
// Every cell carries a `text` member except where noted: a discharge that is right for two
// nulls can still lose a present `text`, and a `text` member is why this tuple is boxed at all.
//
// @falsified-at: b0b5ea15f — interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0.  EXIT-ONLY, and that is
//   not a weak receipt left unnoticed: b1/b2 do not COMPILE on the control (`??` over the boxed
//   spelling is refused there), so no assertion in this file is reachable on that build and the
//   exit is the only channel that CAN move.  It is also why these cells live here rather than
//   beside the `== null` cells: held there, the refusal took the whole file with it and left
//   that close's assertion channel dead — measured `1|1` -> `1|0` -> `1|1` again after the
//   split.  A cell that can refuse the file silences every cell behind it.
// @falsified-by: tests/falsified/a-boxed-tuple-discharges-its-null-like-the-stack-one.patch —
//   interpret exit 1 -> 0, native exit 1 -> 0.
// HOLDS: leak, panic and expectations are equal on both trees, on both backends — falsify prints
//   only the channels that MOVE and requires HERE to be clean.  The two neighbouring cures the
//   cells reject: typing the coalesce result as the NON-null stack tuple passes b1 and b2 while
//   making the subject unbox nullable members into non-null elements, which is the store
//   direction `(N-Store)` refuses; and widening `unboxes_stored_tuple` itself, rather than
//   choosing the result type, would reach the store positions that share it — c1 and c2 are the
//   cells that would still pass under either, which is why they are controls and not evidence.

fn boxed(v: vector<(integer, text)>, i: integer) -> (integer?, text?) { return v[i]; }
fn first<T>(v: vector<T>) -> T? { if len(v) > 0 { return v[0]; } return null; }

fn b1() {
  v: vector<(integer, text)> = [(1, "a"), (2, "bb")];
  q = boxed(v, 5);
  x = q ?? (0, "d");
  assert("{x.0} {x.1}" == "0 d", "b1 `??` over a boxed absent tuple takes the default: {x.0} {x.1}");
}

fn b2() {
  v: vector<(integer, text)> = [(1, "a"), (2, "bb")];
  p = boxed(v, 1);
  y = p ?? (0, "d");
  assert("{y.0} {y.1}" == "2 bb", "b2 `??` over a boxed PRESENT tuple keeps it: {y.0} {y.1}");
}

fn c1() {
  v: vector<(integer, integer)> = [(1, 10), (2, 20)];
  w: (integer?, integer?) = v[5];
  a = w ?? (0, 0);
  u: (integer?, integer?) = v[1];
  b = u ?? (0, 0);
  assert("{a.0} {a.1} {b.0} {b.1}" == "0 0 2 20", "c1 the STACK home is unchanged: {a.0} {a.1} {b.0} {b.1}");
}

fn c2() {
  e: vector<(integer, text)> = [];
  f = first(e);
  x = f ?? (0, "d");
  nv: vector<(integer, text)> = [(4, "d2")];
  g = first(nv);
  y = g ?? (0, "zz");
  assert("{x.0} {x.1} {y.0} {y.1}" == "0 d 4 d2", "c2 the generic `T?` route is unchanged: {x.0} {x.1} {y.0} {y.1}");
}

fn main() {
  b1();
  b2();
  c1();
  c2();
}
